body:
Nice work on this — the simulate-before-send and per-leg receipt waits look solid, and the EIP-1271 fallback is a good touch.

One concrete suggestion for tests/batch_settlement_parity.rs: anchor the EIP-712 derivation against viem (the TypeScript signing library the official x402 client SDK uses to sign vouchers). The current parity tests assert internal consistency — different salts/chains produce different ids, sign+recover round-trips, etc. — which is necessary but doesn't prove that alloy_sol_types' typed-data hash equals viem's hashTypedData byte-for-byte. If they ever diverge (an off-by-one in a type string, a domain-field reorder, an address checksum quirk inside struct encoding), internal consistency happily passes while signatures produced by a real client SDK fail to verify against this facilitator.

I implemented batch-settlement independently in Rust as a hand-rolled keccak256 + abi.encode path and pinned a small set of golden tests against vectors produced with viem. Two of those — the channelId and the voucher digest for a fully concrete ChannelConfig / voucher pair — drop straight into this test file and give cross-implementation parity (viem ↔ alloy ↔ a third hand-rolled implementation, all agreeing on the same 32 bytes). One byte off in any type string, the domain, or the struct encoding changes either hash, so the two assertions transitively pin the typehashes too.

#[test]
fn channel_id_matches_viem_reference_vector() {
    // Vector generated with viem `hashTypedData` over the canonical
    // `x402 Batch Settlement` EIP-712 domain on Base Sepolia
    // (chainId 84532). Independently reproduced by a hand-rolled
    // keccak256+abi.encode implementation; both equal the constant
    // below — so the assertion pins `alloy_sol_types` against viem.
    let cfg = ChannelConfig {
        payer: "0x70997970C51812dc3A010C7d01b50e0d17dc79C8".parse().unwrap(),
        payer_authorizer: "0x70997970C51812dc3A010C7d01b50e0d17dc79C8".parse().unwrap(),
        receiver: "0x19ee5100D3a1e687F85B952bd3FbEc108Ab6A8d7".parse().unwrap(),
        receiver_authorizer: "0xd407e409E34E0b9afb99EcCeb609bDbcD5e7f1bf".parse().unwrap(),
        token: "0x036CbD53842c5426634e7929541eC2318f3dCF7e".parse().unwrap(),
        withdraw_delay: 900,
        salt: B256::ZERO,
    };
    assert_eq!(
        compute_channel_id(&cfg, BASE_SEPOLIA_CHAIN_ID),
        b256!("0x5fafb915f0dbee350d7f84d91802dea47e8e3a71929c3cd79da161c291fb28bd"),
    );
}

#[test]
fn voucher_digest_matches_viem_reference_vector() {
    // Same vector source: viem `hashTypedData` over
    // `Voucher(bytes32 channelId,uint128 maxClaimableAmount)` under the
    // `x402 Batch Settlement` domain on Base Sepolia. The channel id is
    // the one pinned by `channel_id_matches_viem_reference_vector`;
    // max_claimable_amount = 1000.
    let channel_id =
        b256!("0x5fafb915f0dbee350d7f84d91802dea47e8e3a71929c3cd79da161c291fb28bd");
    assert_eq!(
        compute_voucher_digest(channel_id, U128::from(1_000u128), BASE_SEPOLIA_CHAIN_ID),
        b256!("0xa2874adbecca0abb1884b4ac1c100e3906d25208ad0c9e6a8fcf9790ccfa2246"),
    );
}
Reproducible source vectors and a wider parity suite (signature recovery, full verify_voucher round-trip, claim/settle wire-shape) are in [Adelagric/x402-batch-settlement](https://github.com/Adelagric/x402-batch-settlement) — the same project I opened [#92](https://github.com/Adelagric/x402-batch-settlement/issues/92) about. Happy to send these in as a small follow-up PR against your branch if that's easier than copy-pasting.
